WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.100
Welcome to Divisibility and Remainders.

00:00:02.660 --> 00:00:05.500
Some Australian Mathematics Competition problems look as if

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:06.800
they need hundreds of calculations.

00:00:07.900 --> 00:00:08.680
Often they do not.

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:12.260
The useful idea is to keep only what matters after dividing:

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.560
the remainder. Let's see it in an easy problem, a medium

00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:17.180
problem, and a hard one.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.740
Easy problem. Is two thousand, four hundred and seventy-three

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.060
divisible by three?

00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:25.020
You could divide the whole number.

00:00:25.500 --> 00:00:26.740
Instead, look at its digits.

00:00:27.460 --> 00:00:29.840
Why should adding the digits tell us anything about

00:00:29.840 --> 00:00:30.480
divisibility?

00:00:31.580 --> 00:00:35.100
Every ten, hundred or thousand leaves remainder one when

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:36.180
divided by three.

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:39.320
So the digit sum has the same remainder as the original

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.640
number. Two plus four plus seven plus three equals sixteen.

00:00:44.340 --> 00:00:45.860
Sixteen leaves remainder one.

00:00:46.420 --> 00:00:48.220
The number is not divisible by three.

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:53.450
Medium problem. A display repeats six symbols in this order:

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:58.570
triangle, square, circle, star, diamond, hexagon.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:01:02.357
Which symbol is at position one thousand, seven hundred and

00:01:02.357 --> 00:01:05.610
thirty-nine? Never try drawing thousands of symbols.

00:01:06.030 --> 00:01:08.770
Draw one cycle and give each position a number.

00:01:09.810 --> 00:01:14.690
One thousand, seven hundred and thirty-nine equals six times

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.550
two hundred and eighty-nine, plus five.

00:01:17.090 --> 00:01:20.090
There are two hundred and eighty-nine complete cycles, with

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:21.650
five positions left over.

00:01:22.170 --> 00:01:23.530
The fifth symbol is the diamond.

00:01:24.170 --> 00:01:27.510
A remainder of zero would mean the last, or sixth, symbol.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:32.960
Hard problem. Find the smallest three-digit whole number that

00:01:32.960 --> 00:01:36.300
leaves remainder four when divided by five, leaves remainder

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:39.540
six when divided by seven, and is divisible by nine.

00:01:40.220 --> 00:01:42.920
Three conditions look difficult until you notice the pattern:

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.820
both remainders are one less than the divisor.

00:01:46.910 --> 00:01:48.210
Call the number N.

00:01:48.670 --> 00:01:51.770
Then N plus one must be a multiple of both five

00:01:51.770 --> 00:01:55.870
and seven, so N plus one is thirty-five times a whole

00:01:55.870 --> 00:02:00.290
number k. This gives N equals thirty-five k minus one.

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:03.750
We have replaced two conditions with one useful expression.

00:02:04.940 --> 00:02:07.200
We also need N divisible by nine.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.000
So thirty-five k must leave remainder one when divided by

00:02:11.000 --> 00:02:15.040
nine. Thirty-five leaves remainder eight, which is the same as

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:17.920
minus one. Thus k leaves remainder eight.

00:02:18.240 --> 00:02:21.745
The smallest positive choice is eight, giving N equals two

00:02:21.745 --> 00:02:22.420
hundred and seventy-nine.

00:02:23.540 --> 00:02:26.920
Check: the remainders are four and six, and its digits sum

00:02:26.920 --> 00:02:27.460
to eighteen.

00:02:28.930 --> 00:02:33.230
Notice the three levels: a digit sum, a repeating cycle, and

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.230
a number satisfying several restrictions.

00:02:36.190 --> 00:02:39.330
The same tool connects them: simplify to a remainder without

00:02:39.330 --> 00:02:40.810
losing the original conditions.

00:02:41.650 --> 00:02:42.470
Now take your turn.

00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:46.250
Start the lesson problems, explain the key step, and check

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:46.990
each result.

